WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.591 --> 00:00:04.791
I've noticed that almost all the really
good machine learning practitioners tend

2
00:00:04.791 --> 00:00:08.153
to have a very sophisticated
understanding of bias and variance.

3
00:00:08.153 --> 00:00:11.207
Bias and variance is one of those
concepts that's easy to learn but

4
00:00:11.207 --> 00:00:12.250
difficult to master.

5
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.666
Even if you think you've seen
the basic concepts of bias and

6
00:00:15.666 --> 00:00:18.960
variance is often more nuanced
to it than you'd expect.

7
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.386
In the deep learning era, another trend
is that there's been less discussion of

8
00:00:23.386 --> 00:00:25.876
what's called the bias variance trade off.

9
00:00:25.876 --> 00:00:28.856
You might have heard this thing called
the bias variance trade off, but

10
00:00:28.856 --> 00:00:31.204
in the deep learning era,
there's less of a trade off.

11
00:00:31.204 --> 00:00:34.505
So we still talk about bias,
we still talk about variance, but

12
00:00:34.505 --> 00:00:37.259
we just talk less about
the bias variance trade off.

13
00:00:37.259 --> 00:00:40.365
Let's see what this means.

14
00:00:40.365 --> 00:00:42.617
Let's say you have a data
set that looks like this.

15
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:44.928
If you fit a straight line to the data,

16
00:00:44.928 --> 00:00:47.899
maybe you get a logistic
regression fit to that.

17
00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:52.139
This is not a very good fit to the data,
and so there's a cause of high bias.

18
00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:56.615
Or we say that this is
underfitting the data.

19
00:00:56.615 --> 00:01:01.494
On the opposite end, if you fit
an incredibly complex classifier,

20
00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:03.640
maybe a deep neural network.

21
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.101
Or a new network with
a lot of hidden units,

22
00:01:07.101 --> 00:01:10.188
maybe you can fit the data perfectly.

23
00:01:10.188 --> 00:01:12.108
But that doesn't look
like a great fit either.

24
00:01:12.108 --> 00:01:15.199
So this is a classifier
with high variance,

25
00:01:15.199 --> 00:01:17.631
and this is overfitting the data.

26
00:01:17.631 --> 00:01:21.953
And there might be some classifier
in between with a medium level of

27
00:01:21.953 --> 00:01:24.974
complexity that maybe
fits a curve like that.

28
00:01:24.974 --> 00:01:27.137
That looks like a much more
reasonable fit to the data.

29
00:01:27.137 --> 00:01:31.725
So that's the, and call that just
right somewhere in each tree.

30
00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:36.000
So in a 2d example like this,
with just two features, x1 and x2,

31
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.455
you can plot the data and
visualize bias and variance.

32
00:01:39.455 --> 00:01:42.879
In high dimensional problems,
you can't plot the data and

33
00:01:42.879 --> 00:01:44.942
visualize the decision boundary.

34
00:01:44.942 --> 00:01:48.291
Instead, there are couple different
metrics that we'll look at to try to

35
00:01:48.291 --> 00:01:49.758
understand bias and variance.

36
00:01:49.758 --> 00:01:53.544
So, continuing our example of
cat picture classification,

37
00:01:53.544 --> 00:01:57.637
where that's a positive example and
that's a negative example.

38
00:01:57.637 --> 00:02:00.932
The two key numbers to look
at to understand bias and

39
00:02:00.932 --> 00:02:06.460
variance will be the trading set error and
the dev set, or the development set error.

40
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.683
So, for the sake of argument,
let's say that recognizing

41
00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:13.813
cats in pictures is something that
people can do nearly perfectly, right?

42
00:02:13.813 --> 00:02:20.670
And so let's say your trading size
error is 1% and your dev set error is,

43
00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:25.440
for the sake of argument,
let's say, is 11%.

44
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.206
So in this example, you're doing
very well on the training set, but

45
00:02:30.206 --> 00:02:34.248
you're doing relatively poorly
on the development set.

46
00:02:34.248 --> 00:02:37.688
So this looks like you might
have overfit the training set.

47
00:02:37.688 --> 00:02:42.638
That somehow you're not generalizing well
to this holdout cost validation set to

48
00:02:42.638 --> 00:02:43.795
development set.

49
00:02:43.795 --> 00:02:50.653
And so if you have an example like this,
we will say this has high variance.

50
00:02:50.653 --> 00:02:54.824
So by looking at the training set
error and the development set error,

51
00:02:54.824 --> 00:02:59.574
you would be able to render a diagnosis
of your algorithm having high variance.

52
00:02:59.574 --> 00:03:04.166
Now let's say that you measure your
training set in your dev set error and

53
00:03:04.166 --> 00:03:05.980
you get a different result.

54
00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.599
Let's say that your
training set error is 15%.

55
00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:15.792
I'm writing your training set error in
the top row and your dev set error is 16%.

56
00:03:15.792 --> 00:03:21.445
In this case, assuming that
humans achieve roughly 0% error,

57
00:03:21.445 --> 00:03:27.625
that humans can look at these pictures and
just tell if it's cat or not.

58
00:03:27.625 --> 00:03:31.450
Then it looks like the algorithm is not
even doing very well on the training set.

59
00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.721
So if it's not even fitting
the training data, as seen that well,

60
00:03:35.721 --> 00:03:38.102
then this is underfitting the data.

61
00:03:38.102 --> 00:03:41.098
And so this algorithm has high bias.

62
00:03:41.098 --> 00:03:41.934
But in contrast,

63
00:03:41.934 --> 00:03:45.503
this is actually generalizing at
a reasonable level to the dev set, whereas

64
00:03:45.503 --> 00:03:49.316
performance of the dev set is only 1%
worse as performance on the training set.

65
00:03:49.316 --> 00:03:53.866
So this algorithm has a problem of high
bias because it's not even training,

66
00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:56.605
it's not even fitting
the training set well.

67
00:03:56.605 --> 00:04:00.955
This is similar to the leftmost
plot we had on the previous slide.

68
00:04:00.955 --> 00:04:03.367
Now here's another example.

69
00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:06.487
Let's say that you have
15% trading set error.

70
00:04:06.487 --> 00:04:08.127
So that's pretty high bias.

71
00:04:08.127 --> 00:04:13.538
But when you evaluate on a dev set,
it does even worse., maybe it does 30%.

72
00:04:13.538 --> 00:04:18.611
In this case, I would diagnose
this algorithm as having high bias

73
00:04:18.611 --> 00:04:24.160
because it's not doing that well on
the trading set and high variance.

74
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.205
So this is really
the worst of both worlds.

75
00:04:28.205 --> 00:04:34.055
And one last example, if you have 0.5
training set error and 1% dev set error.

76
00:04:34.055 --> 00:04:41.055
Then maybe our users are quite happy that
you have a cad costly with only 1% error,

77
00:04:41.055 --> 00:04:45.060
then this would have low bias and
low variance.

78
00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.005
One subtlety that I'll
just briefly mention, but

79
00:04:48.005 --> 00:04:50.959
we'll leave to a later
video to discuss in detail.

80
00:04:50.959 --> 00:04:55.728
Is that this analysis is predicated
on the assumption that human

81
00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:59.007
level performance gets nearly 0% error.

82
00:04:59.007 --> 00:05:05.897
Or more generally they're the optimal
error, sometimes called Bayes error for

83
00:05:05.897 --> 00:05:10.164
the so
the bayesian optimal error is nearly 0%.

84
00:05:10.164 --> 00:05:13.563
I don't want to go into detail on
this in this particular video.

85
00:05:13.563 --> 00:05:18.542
But it turns out that if the optimal error
or the Bayes error were much higher,

86
00:05:18.542 --> 00:05:19.856
say it were 15%.

87
00:05:19.856 --> 00:05:24.443
Then if you look at this classifier,
15% is actually perfectly reasonable for

88
00:05:24.443 --> 00:05:25.313
training set.

89
00:05:25.313 --> 00:05:30.541
And you wouldn't say it as high bias and
also have pretty low variance.

90
00:05:30.541 --> 00:05:33.138
So the case of how to analyze bias and

91
00:05:33.138 --> 00:05:36.523
variance when no classifier
can do very well.

92
00:05:36.523 --> 00:05:40.691
For example,
if you have really blurry images so

93
00:05:40.691 --> 00:05:46.095
that even a human or just no system
could possibly do very well.

94
00:05:46.095 --> 00:05:49.210
Then maybe Bayes error is much higher.

95
00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:52.269
And then there's some details of
how this analysis will change.

96
00:05:52.269 --> 00:05:55.341
But leaving aside this subtlety for now,

97
00:05:55.341 --> 00:05:59.660
the takeaway is that by looking
at your trading set error.

98
00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:04.282
You can get a sense of how well you're
fitting at least the training data.

99
00:06:04.282 --> 00:06:06.948
And so
that tells you if you have a bias problem.

100
00:06:06.948 --> 00:06:11.851
And then looking at how much higher your
error goes when you go from the training

101
00:06:11.851 --> 00:06:13.050
set to the dev set.

102
00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:16.865
That should give you a sense of
how bad is the variance problem.

103
00:06:16.865 --> 00:06:21.042
So are you doing a good job generalizing
from the training set to the dev set that

104
00:06:21.042 --> 00:06:22.951
gives you a sense of your variance?

105
00:06:22.951 --> 00:06:26.559
All this is under the assumption that
the Bayes error is quite small and

106
00:06:26.559 --> 00:06:30.363
that your train and your death sets
are drawn from the same distribution.

107
00:06:30.363 --> 00:06:34.368
If those assumptions are violated, there's
more sophisticated analysis you could do,

108
00:06:34.368 --> 00:06:36.327
which we'll talk about in the later video.

109
00:06:36.327 --> 00:06:41.135
Now, on the previous slide you saw what
high bias, high variance looks like, and

110
00:06:41.135 --> 00:06:44.837
I guess you have the sense of what
a good classifier looks like.

111
00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:48.040
What does high bias and
high variance looks like?

112
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.339
It's kind of the worst of both worlds.

113
00:06:50.339 --> 00:06:53.889
So you remember we said that
a classifier like this,

114
00:06:53.889 --> 00:06:58.176
a linear classifier, has high bias
because it under fits the data.

115
00:06:58.176 --> 00:07:01.764
So this would be a classifier
that is mostly linear and

116
00:07:01.764 --> 00:07:03.927
therefore under fits the data.

117
00:07:03.927 --> 00:07:05.546
We'll join this in purple.

118
00:07:05.546 --> 00:07:09.776
But if somehow your classifier
does some weird things,

119
00:07:09.776 --> 00:07:14.280
then it's actually overfitting
parts of the data as well.

120
00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:19.748
So the classifier that I drew in purple
has both high bias and high variance.

121
00:07:19.748 --> 00:07:24.230
There's high bias because by
being a mostly linear classifier,

122
00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:28.477
it's just not fitting this
quadratic light shape that well.

123
00:07:28.477 --> 00:07:32.877
But by having too much flexibility in
the middle, it somehow gets this example.

124
00:07:32.877 --> 00:07:36.514
And this example overfits
those two examples as well.

125
00:07:36.514 --> 00:07:40.512
So this classifier kind of has high
bias because it was mostly linear, but

126
00:07:40.512 --> 00:07:43.747
you needed maybe a curve function,
a quadratic function.

127
00:07:43.747 --> 00:07:48.678
And it has high variance because it had
too much flexibility to fit those two

128
00:07:48.678 --> 00:07:52.132
mislabeled outlier examples
in the middle as well.

129
00:07:52.132 --> 00:07:54.675
In case this seems contrived, well, it is.

130
00:07:54.675 --> 00:07:57.916
This example is a little bit
contrived in two dimensions, but

131
00:07:57.916 --> 00:07:59.858
with very high dimensional inputs.

132
00:07:59.858 --> 00:08:02.973
You actually do get things with
high bias in some regions and

133
00:08:02.973 --> 00:08:04.666
high variance in some regions.

134
00:08:04.666 --> 00:08:09.583
And so it is possible to get cross files
like this in high dimensional inputs

135
00:08:09.583 --> 00:08:11.389
that seem less contrived.

136
00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:15.741
So, to summarize, you've seen how by
looking at your algorithm's error

137
00:08:15.741 --> 00:08:19.416
on the training set and
your algorithm's error on the dev set.

138
00:08:19.416 --> 00:08:23.736
You can try to diagnose whether it has
problem of high bias or high variance, or

139
00:08:23.736 --> 00:08:25.514
maybe both, or maybe neither.

140
00:08:25.514 --> 00:08:28.874
And depending on whether your algorithm
suffers from bias or variance,

141
00:08:28.874 --> 00:08:31.642
it turns out that there
are different things you could try.

142
00:08:31.642 --> 00:08:36.171
So in the next video, I want to present
to you what I call a basic recipe for

143
00:08:36.171 --> 00:08:37.447
machine learning.

144
00:08:37.447 --> 00:08:41.713
That lets you more systematically try
to improve your algorithm depending on

145
00:08:41.713 --> 00:08:44.283
whether as high bias or
high variance issues.

146
00:08:44.283 --> 00:08:45.730
So let's go on to the next video.